 bring D into discussion, but must give a vote. We could change our vote to D, or keep C, or shift to another. We need to make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing prior discussion.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate is best overall based on evidence. Let's re-evaluate all candidates with all evidence now:\n\nCandidate A:\n- Strengths: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities.\n- Concerns: regarded as show\u2011off, not open to new ideas, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized.\n- Unknown: stress resilience, attention & information accuracy, professional communication, adaptability/feedback orientation.\n\nCandidate B:\n- Strengths: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather conditions, excellent computer skills.\n- Concerns: pretentious, adopts wrong tone when communicating, grumpy, uncooperative.\n\nCandidate C:\n- Strengths: 100% reliable, creates positive crew atmosphere, makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology.\n- Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further his education.\n\nCandidate D:\n- Strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously.\n- Concerns: regarded as arrogant/know\u2011it\u2011all, relatively weak leadership skills, hot temper, moody, loner.\n\nWe need to weigh criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention/accuracy, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's score roughly:\n\nReliability: A unknown, B very conscientious (high), C 100% reliable (high), D takes responsibility seriously (moderate). So C highest.\n\nStress resilience: A unknown, B handles stress very well (high), C keeps calm in crisis (high), D responds to unexpected events adequately (moderate). So B and C high.\n\nTechnical competence: A sees complex connections, spatial vision (good), B good at assessing weather, computer skills (good), C understands complicated technology (high), D solves problems extremely well, concentration (good). So C high.\n\nDecision quality: A anticipates dangerous situations (good), C makes correct decisions quickly (high), B unknown, D solves problems extremely well (good).\n\nAttention & accuracy: Not much info. B has \"excellent computer skills\" maybe implies attention, but also \"grumpy\" not relevant. No explicit.\n\nCrew cooperation: A not open to new ideas, show-off (negative), B pretentious, uncooperative (negative), C creates positive crew atmosphere (positive), D arrogant, loner (negative).\n\nProfessional communication: A not good at taking criticism (negative), B wrong tone (negative), C difficulty communicating ideas (negative), D hot temper (negative). All have issues.\n\nResponsibility: A leadership (positive), B conscientious (positive), C reliability (positive), D takes responsibility seriously (positive).\n\nAdaptability/feedback: A not open to new ideas (negative), B grumpy (negative), C not willing to further education (negative), D hot temper, moody (negative). All have issues.\n\nThus candidate C stands out for reliability, crew atmosphere, decision quality, calmness, technical competence. The main concerns: communication difficulty, egocentric, unwillingness to further education. Those affect crew cooperation and adaptability. But his positive crew atmosphere suggests he can still work well? However difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric may hinder crew cooperation. But \"creates a positive atmosphere with his crew\" suggests good cooperation. So maybe his communication issues are not fatal.\n\nCandidate B has strong stress resilience and conscientiousness, but communication tone issues and uncooperative. That could be problematic.\n\nCandidate A has unique anticipation and leadership but unknown stress resilience and many attitude issues.\n\nCandidate D has responsibility and problem solving but negative crew cooperation.\n\nThus candidate C seems strongest overall despite concerns. So as Agent 1, we could maintain our vote for C, but we need to respond to the arguments from Agent 2 and 3. We can argue that C's reliability and crew atmosphere outweigh B's stress handling and A's anticipation. Also we can bring D into discussion as a point but still vote C.\n\nThus we will keep vote Candidate C, and argue that C's combination of reliability, calmness, decision quality, and positive crew atmosphere is most aligned with criteria, and that his communication issues are less severe than the other candidates' more serious cooperation problems.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: mention Agent 2's points about A, Agent 3's points about B, and our own earlier recommendation for C. Provide evidence: 100% reliable, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Address concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, but argue that his reliability and crew atmosphere mitigate.\n\nThus produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate C."}
